From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/imagination: scale the core clock with the firmware's
 utilisation

Add devfreq to drm/imagination: an OPP table on the "core" clock, an optional
"power" supply set with it, the simple_ondemand governor fed from the
firmware's utilisation data, and a devfreq cooling device. A device tree
without operating-points-v2 is unaffected: the driver does nothing then.

Load. Busy time is the firmware's ACTIVE time, total time the firmware's sum
of IDLE, ACTIVE and BLOCKED time over the same interval, as the vendor driver
divides it. Both are in the clock of the timer correlation
(CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW, `drm-imagination-publish-the-timer-correlation-on-monotonic-raw`),
whose conversion changes with every rate, and that conversion cancels out of
the quotient. stats_counters[] only grow when the firmware leaves a state, so
the time in the current state is added from last_word's stamp. last_word is
read before and after the counters; if it moved, the read is repeated. If the
firmware's time did not advance the interval counts as idle with the wall
time as total. A sample that cannot be trusted -- no consistent read, counters
that went backwards because the firmware was reset, a GPU that is
runtime-suspended -- counts as idle and starts a new baseline, and busy is
capped at total. A bad sample can therefore lower the clock for one period,
never raise it.

Rate change. dev_pm_opp_set_rate() orders supply and clock. Afterwards the
firmware is told: the rate goes into SYSINIT and the runtime config, which the
firmware reads at boot. A running firmware gets a new timer correlation entry
from pvr_fw_time_corr_rate_changed(), because ROGUE_CR_TIMER counts core
clock cycles and the firmware converts its timestamps with the factor of the
newest entry: the entry starts at the current CR and OS time (not before the
firmware's own time, so timestamps do not go backwards) with the factor of
the new rate. Then ROGUE_FWIF_KCCB_CMD_CORECLKSPEEDCHANGE follows. All of that
only happens if the GPU is already active (pm_runtime_get_if_active()) and no
reset holds reset_sem (down_read_trylock()), so the governor never powers the
GPU and never waits for a reset; a reset publishes its own correlation when it
restarts the firmware. A firmware can still have booted with the previous
rate: a runtime resume that took clk_lock first (pm_runtime_get_if_active()
refuses until it is done), or a reset that restarted the firmware and still
holds reset_sem. A change is therefore marked until the firmware is told, and
the first watchdog tick (every 500 ms) that finds the GPU in use passes it on
with pvr_devfreq_sync_fw(); a GPU that goes idle first is powered off and
boots with the rate from SYSINIT. The entry is taken after the clock switch
returned, so a timestamp written in that short window is converted with the
old factor. The watchdog's periodic refresh keeps one entry from covering
more than a few seconds.

clk_lock serialises the writers of the rate and the correlation: the
governor and pvr_power_fw_enable(), which now stores the current rate before it
starts the firmware.

Lifetime. devfreq_add_device(), not the devm variant, so the governor is
removed in pvr_device_fini() before the firmware structures it reads are
freed. A failure to set up devfreq is reported and not fatal: the GPU keeps
the rate its clock has, and the device tree has to make that a safe one.
-EPROBE_DEFER from the supply is the exception and fails the probe, so the
device probes again once the supply is there instead of running unscaled.

Polling every 200 ms. simple_ondemand gets an upthreshold of 45 and a
downdifferential of 5, as panfrost and panthor use, instead of its 90 and 5:
a process that submits a job and waits for it keeps the GPU busy enough at
the lowest rate to stay inside the default band of 85 to 90, so the rate
never rises.

The driver maps the runtime config the way it maps the other structures the
host writes.

Assisted-by: Claude
---
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/imagination/Kconfig
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/imagination/Kconfig
